---
title: Building a site
order: 40
description: The build pipeline, configuration and preview loop.
---

Building is a single command. From a documentation root containing
`quaydoc.toml` (optional) and any number of `.qd` or `.md` files:

```console
$ python3 -m quaydoc.cli build . -o /tmp/site
ok: built 9 page(s), 3,412 words, 47.1 KB in 0.21s
```

## What a build does

1. Discover source files (skipping the output directory, hidden
   directories, and `.quayignore` patterns),
2. parse every page: front matter, blocks, inline markup, includes,
3. assign heading anchors and resolve reference targets,
4. render each page and its navigation,
5. write the pages plus `assets/`, `search_index.json`, `sitemap.xml` and
   `feed.xml`.

## Configuration

`quaydoc.toml` holds the settings:

```toml
[site]
title = "Quaydoc Manual"
base_url = "https://docs.example.net/quaydoc"
pretty_urls = true

[build]
output_dir = "site"
search = true
rss = true
default_lang = "python"
```

Unknown keys are reported as warnings by `quaydoc check`. The full key list
is in the configuration reference.

## Preview loop

`quaydoc serve site` starts a local server on port 8000. When you are
editing sources, `quaydoc watch .` rebuilds the site whenever a source file
changes:

```console
$ python3 -m quaydoc.cli watch .
```

## Archiving

`quaydoc build --archive manual.tar.gz` bundles the rendered output into a
gzip tar that reproduces the site root on extraction.
